window.docContents[75].resolve({"/Blog-posts/The-Curse-of-Hardware----A-language-centric-problem-framing/#The-Den--Blog-posts--The-Curse-of-Hardware----A-language-centric-problem-framing":{"contents":"Motivation\n\nHardware projects come with a lot of headaches in my experience that software projects don't. In my early uni days I was involved in a project to create (and then embezzle) an E-paper laptop -- ordering circuit components and soldering them together with a friend.\n\nI'm terrible with my hands; I'd often burn out components by running current in the wrong direction, or doing something or other and then that dreaded smoke would rise from the circuitry to tell you that you probably needed to buy a new one. In our case, that meant looking online for who was still in stock, begging for moar monies from the school, and then waiting for the thing to arrive in a month or so. It happened at least twice in that project alone.\n\nIf only it was a software project! In software you could bork your entire installation, wipe it clean and restart at the cost of a few hours and no money at all. Over the course of our embezzlement, my friend and I coined the term the Curse of Hardware.\n\nWhat is the Curse of Hardware?\n\nSimply put, the Curse of Hardware is when you want to write a program in some language that forgoes certain undesirable features; but the compile target that your language must compile into demands that your high-level language includes those features.\n\nIn the previous example, the curse of hardware was the friction it took to do anything with physical metal and wires, compared to just writing code and being able to fuck up and re-write / re-run things effortlessly and for free.\n\nBut meh; that's boring. Can we generalize it?\n\nIn particular:\n\n* We were operating in the domain of: Physically working with circuitry* We wanted to get a new circuit component* We wanted to avoid having to pay money or deal with shipping\n\n\"Physically working with circuitry\" is a domain; when physically working with circuitry, one has to deal with concepts such as\n\nThink of reality as a CPU. You want to:\n\n* Do something on that CPU -- i.e. write a program that will run on that CPU* Achieve some goal -- i.e. the program end-state fulfils some target condition* While avoiding some other things -- i.e. the program itself must be written while avoiding some patterns\n\n","context":"The Den\u0009Blog posts","header":"3.1. The Curse of Hardware -- A language-centric problem framing","id":"/Blog-posts/The-Curse-of-Hardware----A-language-centric-problem-framing/#The-Den--Blog-posts--The-Curse-of-Hardware----A-language-centric-problem-framing"}});